          body: "## Your first corpus\n\nThis takes about five minutes on any project folder, ideally one in git.\n\n## 1. Open the project\n\nSign in, then **File → Open Project…** (`Ctrl+Shift+O`) and choose the project folder.\n\n## 2. Create its corpus\n\nA folder with no corpus asks first: *This folder has no corpus yet*. Choose **Create corpus**. Corpus reads the project, writes `.xeno/corpus/` inside it, derives its first checks from what is already there and runs them. Examples of derived checks:\n\n| Derived claim | Checked by reading |\n|---|---|\n| The declared version is *x.y.z* | `package.json` |\n| A licence is declared | `package.json` |\n| The package is marked private | `package.json` |\n| No dependency points at a local file | `package.json` |\n| The tree has no uncommitted changes | `git status` |\n| The repository has a remote | `git remote` |\n| The tree contains no junctions or symlinks | the file system |\n\nNothing outside the folder changes. Anything already false on day one is recorded in the **baseline**, so it shows as lapsed without making every later pulse fail. See [Tenets and verdicts](/docs/corpus/tenets-and-verdicts).\n\n## 3. Read the answer\n\nThe [Shape canvas](/docs/corpus/shape) opens. Choose **False now** at the top left to see only what is false. Select a card: the inspector on the right shows what was expected, what was observed, the exact command that checked it, and a suggested next action.\n\n## 4. Add a claim of your own\n\nPress `N` (or **+ New tenet**). Write the claim in plain words, choose **Write my own** under *Check that could disprove it*, and enter a command that prints one value as its last line, for example:\n\n```\nnode -e \"console.log(require('./package.json').engines.node)\"\n```\n\nSet **Expect** to the value that means the claim holds, choose **Run a test**, then **Add tenet**. A draft can only be saved after its exact fields have been tested.\n\n## 5. Ask a question\n\nPress `Ctrl+K` and type a question that ends with `?`, such as *which Node version do we support?* The answer names the claim and its state, with the evidence behind it.\n\n## 6. Save a project file (optional)\n\n**File → Save Project File (.xcorpus)** writes a small file you can double-click to reopen this project. See [Opening projects](/docs/corpus/opening-a-project).",
