          title: "Tenets and verdicts",
          description: "Claims, their checks, the append-only verdict log, freshness, the baseline and versions.",
          body: "## Tenets and verdicts\n\nA **tenet** has:\n\n| Part | Example |\n|---|---|\n| **ID** | `api#port` (subject `#` name) |\n| **Claim** | *The API listens on port 8080* |\n| **Check** | a command, or a person's attestation |\n| **Expect** | `8080`, or a comparison such as *at most* `600` |\n| **Owner** | the person or team who fixes it when it lapses |\n| **Cadence** | how often it should be checked (default daily) |\n\n## Verdicts are never rewritten\n\nEach run of a check appends a verdict to `.xeno/corpus/verdicts/`. Nothing edits or deletes one, and each log is hash-chained, so editing a past verdict is detectable (`corpus integrity`). The **Verdicts** view lists them newest first, and the pulse history strip on the canvas lets you view the corpus *as of* any earlier pulse.\n\n## Freshness\n\nA held claim does not stay held forever. Each tenet has three clocks:\n\n| Clock | Default | After it passes |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Cadence** | 24 hours | the claim is due to be checked again |\n| **Warn after** | 72 hours | the claim is shown as getting old |\n| **Expire after** | 240 hours | the old result no longer counts; the claim reads **Unknown** |\n\n## The baseline\n\nWhen a corpus is created, anything already false is accepted into `baseline.json`. Those claims still show as **lapsed**, but they do not make a pulse fail. A lapse that appears later is **new**, and a pulse reports it with exit code 1. Once a baselined claim is fixed it leaves the baseline, so it fails loudly if it breaks again. `corpus baseline show` lists the accepted lapses; `corpus baseline drop TENET --reason R` removes one.\n\n## Versions, never edits\n\nChanging a tenet's claim, check or expectation saves a **new version**; the old one stays readable under **Version history** in the inspector. Corpus never deletes a tenet: pressing `Delete` explains that a tenet is *superseded* instead (`corpus tenet supersede ID --by OTHER --reason R`), and its verdicts stay on record.\n\n## Do not raise the expectation to make a lapse go away\n\nWhen a check fails, the suggested next action is to fix the project, not the claim. Changing the expectation to match the new value retires the check. If the expectation really is out of date, revise the tenet with a reason, or mark the lapse **This lapse is wrong → The expectation is outdated** so the false-alarm rate stays honest.",
