          description: "How a check runs, what makes it Unknown, human attestations, and the safety rules around commands.",
          body: "## Checks\n\nA check (an *assay*) is a command run in the project folder. Its **last line of output** is the observed value, compared with the tenet's expectation.\n\n| Result | When |\n|---|---|\n| **Held** | the command exited 0 and its value matches |\n| **Lapsed** | the command exited 0 and its value does not match |\n| **Unknown** | the command failed, timed out (2 minutes), printed nothing, or printed something the comparison cannot evaluate |\n\nA check that could not run is **never** recorded as held. *Unknown* means Corpus does not know, and it says so.\n\n## Comparisons\n\n**Expect** is either an exact value (*equals*) or a number with *less than*, *at most*, *more than* or *at least*. On the command line: `--expect 8080`, or `--expect-op lte --expect-value 600`.\n\n## Human attestations\n\nSome claims have no command that can check them (*our privacy policy was reviewed by counsel*). Choose **Human attestation** in the tenet form: a person vouches for the statement and it reads **Attested**, never held, until it expires (default 30 days). Re-attest from the inspector or with `corpus tenet attest ID`.\n\n## Safety\n\n- **Checks run with your permissions and are not sandboxed.** Read a check in the inspector before you run it. The form says so next to the command field.\n- **A command found in a document is never run.** When Corpus reads claims out of your documents (`corpus ingest`, or the Document view), any command it finds becomes a *proposal* for a person to review. It runs only after someone approves it.\n- **A tenet is never saved on a check nobody has run.** The form, and `corpus tenet new`, run the exact draft once first.\n\n## Checks on a PC without Node.js\n\nSeveral checks that **Create corpus** derives call `node`. In the app, and through its `corpus` command, they run on XENO Corpus's own runtime when Node.js is not installed. An installed Node.js is always used first.",
