          body: "## Documents and claims\n\nProjects already describe themselves in prose: `CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `README.md`, design notes. Corpus registers these as **documents** and marks which sentences make a checkable claim.\n\n## The Document view\n\nPress `2` (or **Document**). Each agent-context file in the project gets a tab. Lines that carry a claim are marked by state: lapsed, held, unknown, no check, or rule. The header shows coverage, for example *coverage 14 of 31 fact lines*.\n\nBanner chips flag the whole file: **LAPSED** when a claim in it is false, **LONG** above 600 lines (agents are likely to skip parts of it), **GENERATED** when Corpus wrote it.\n\nSelect a line without a check and choose **Write a tenet** to bind it to one.\n\n## Proposals\n\n`corpus ingest FILE.md` reads claims out of a document and writes them as **proposals**. Nothing is checked or run at that point. Review them in the app (the inspector's **Accept as tenet** or **Dismiss**, with a reason) or on the command line:\n\n```\ncorpus proposal list\ncorpus proposal show ID\ncorpus proposal approve ID\ncorpus proposal reject ID\n```\n\n## Drift\n\nWhen a document names files or symbols in your code, Corpus remembers them. `corpus drift` reports documents whose code references changed since they were last confirmed (exit 1 when something drifted). `corpus drift ack DOC --reason R` confirms the document is still right.\n\n## Rules\n\nSome lines are rules for agents rather than facts (*never force-push*). Corpus carries those to agents word for word, never paraphrased. See [Connecting AI agents](/docs/corpus/connecting-agents).",
