          description: "Give Claude Code, Cursor or Codex the current truth of the project before they act.",
          body: "## Connecting AI agents\n\nAn agent reads `CLAUDE.md` and trusts it. Corpus makes sure what it reads is current: false claims first, rules word for word, and nothing quietly out of date.\n\n`corpus adopt` sets this up for one agent in one project. It shows what it would change first, and changes nothing until you add `--write`:\n\n```\ncorpus adopt --for claude-code\ncorpus adopt --for claude-code --write\n```\n\n| Agent | What `adopt` writes |\n|---|---|\n| `claude-code` | `.claude/settings.json`: a hook at session start that hands the agent the current corpus, and a guard before shell commands. `.mcp.json`: the Corpus MCP server |\n| `cursor` | `.cursor/mcp.json`: the Corpus MCP server |\n| `codex` | `.codex/config.toml`: the Corpus MCP server |\n\nYour existing configuration is kept. A file Corpus does not understand is refused rather than rewritten. Every change is backed up first; `corpus adopt receipts` lists the backups and `corpus adopt restore --receipt ID --write` restores one.\n\nRun `adopt` with the [`corpus` command](/docs/corpus/command-line) installed from the app, and the hooks it writes use XENO Corpus's own runtime, so they work without Node.js. They also follow the app's sign-in: signed out, the hooks stay quiet, so your agent carries on without the corpus instead of failing, and the MCP server does not start. Signing in again restores both.\n\nConnected an agent with 0.1.4? Run `corpus adopt --for <agent> --write` once more: it upgrades the MCP entry it wrote then, and changes nothing else.\n\n## What the agent gets\n\n- **At session start**, and again when the conversation is compacted, the agent receives the corpus projection: false claims first (never cut for space), rules word for word, then claims that hold. See [The projection](/docs/corpus/projection).\n- **Before a shell command**, the guard checks the command against the corpus's rules. When a rule applies, it tells the agent. **It never blocks a command**: your agent's own permission settings still decide what runs.\n\nTry the guard in the app: **Integrations → Try a command against the guard**. Nothing is executed.\n\n## Teaching search from agents\n\n`corpus agents on` (Claude Code only) adds a read hook and the Corpus search server, so the documents your agent actually opens improve later search rankings, on this PC only. `corpus agents off` removes exactly what `on` added. `later` and `never` record your decision.\n\n## The MCP server\n\nSix read-only tools, for any MCP client:\n\n| Tool | Returns |\n|---|---|\n| `corpus_context` | The projection: rules in force, false claims, unknown claims, held claims. With a query, search results instead |\n| `corpus_tenet` | One claim with its latest verdict; with `as_of`, what it was at that time |\n| `corpus_lapsed` | Claims that are false now, most recently checked first |\n| `corpus_consumers` | Everything that references a subject: claims, documents, rules |\n| `corpus_can_ship` | A release gate: ship only if every claim about a subject holds and is fresh |\n| `corpus_unmanaged` | Registered documents no claim is bound to |\n\nThe server runs with `corpus mcp --from PROJECT`. **Settings → API & MCP** shows the exact command for the open project.",
