          body: "## The corpus command\n\nEverything the app does is also a command. The app ships it, with its own runtime, so it needs no Node.js.\n\n## Install it\n\nIn XENO Corpus, choose **Help → Install 'corpus' Command…** and **Add to PATH**. Open a **new** terminal (PowerShell, Command Prompt or Git Bash) and run:\n\n```\ncorpus --help\n```\n\nOnly one entry is added to your user PATH; every other entry is left exactly as it was. **Help → Remove 'corpus' Command** takes it off again, and uninstalling the app removes it too. Updating the app keeps it.\n\n## It uses the app's sign-in\n\nThe command runs while XENO Corpus is signed in with an account that has access, the same as the app window. It follows the app's offline rule: if the app has not confirmed your account online for 14 days, open it while connected. Signed out, a command prints why and exits with code `6`, and nothing runs. `corpus --help` always works.\n\n## Where it acts\n\nMost commands act on the corpus of the folder you are in: Corpus walks up from the current folder to find `.xeno/corpus/`. Pass `--from PROJECT` to act on another project. Add `--json` for machine-readable output.\n\n## A first session\n\n```\ncd C:\\code\\my-project\ncorpus init --dry-run\ncorpus init\ncorpus pulse\ncorpus ask \"what version do we ship?\"\ncorpus context --budget 4000\n```\n\n`init --dry-run` shows the checks it would derive and changes nothing.\n\n## Help for every command\n\n`corpus <command> --help` prints the usage of any command. See the [command reference](/docs/corpus/command-reference) and [exit codes](/docs/corpus/exit-codes).",
